## Authentication

Create an API key in MyKinsta under *Company settings → API keys* and pass it through the
`KINSTA_API_KEY` environment variable (or the `api_key` argument). Every key belongs to one
company; the provider looks it up from the key unless `company_id` (or `KINSTA_COMPANY_ID`)
is set. Keys can expire: a `401` is reported as an invalid, expired or revoked key.

## Lifecycle behavior

- Kinsta runs most changes asynchronously. The provider waits for each operation to finish
  and reports Kinsta's reason when one fails.
- `kinsta_site` and `kinsta_environment` default to `deletion_protection = true`, because a
  delete removes files and databases and Kinsta has no undo. Apply `false` before destroying.
- WordPress installation arguments (admin user, title, language, plugins) only apply when a
  site or environment is created. Kinsta never returns them, so they are not tracked as drift
  and changing them later does not replace anything.
- An environment always has a primary domain, so destroying `kinsta_primary_domain` leaves
  it in place. Move the primary domain away from a `kinsta_site_domain` before destroying it.

